/*
RMSTEST_39 - the RAW elevation seed distribution, without growth or merging
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 11(a)

THE PROBLEM WITH EVERY ELEVATION MEASUREMENT SO FAR
RMSTEST_22a, 32 and 35 all measured ELEVATED TILES. Elevated tiles are what a
seed grew into, so every number this project has for the diagonal bias - 18:1,
7.16:1, the 29:1 interior and 3.7:1 border figures - is a seed distribution seen
through a growth blur, and through merging: RMSTEST_35 declared 500 clumps and
returned only ~283 components per run, so nearly half of them ran together.

That matters for item 11(a) specifically. We know the bias weakens within ~12
tiles of the map edge, we know the width does not scale with map size, and we
know it is NOT seed exclusion (component centroids appear at edge distance 0)
and NOT spillover across the diagonal (only 0.7-3.0% of disfavoured tiles sit in
a component that also holds favoured tiles). Both candidate mechanisms are dead,
and what is left to ask is whether the SEEDS themselves are less biased near the
boundary, or whether seeding is uniform and something in growth produces the
effect.

A grown-tile measurement cannot separate those. This one can.

METHOD
Set the per-clump share to the measured clump floor, so each clump IS its seed
plus the minimum growth the engine always applies:

  number_of_clumps 100, number_of_tiles 600  ->  6 tiles per clump

100 clumps of ~6 tiles on a 200 map is 1.5% coverage against RMSTEST_35's 6.2%,
so merging should be rare and each connected component is one seed to within a
tile or two. Five runs gives ~500 near-clean seeds.

Everything else matches RMSTEST_22a and 35 exactly - GRASS base, no player
lands, spacing 1, create_elevation 1 - so the only change is the clump/tile
split. Nothing about the bias itself is varied.

READ IT WITH
  Connected components of elevation > 0; take each centroid as a seed.
  Then, for seeds: the favoured:disfavoured split, and that split PROFILED
  AGAINST DISTANCE FROM THE NEAREST MAP EDGE, which is the reading.
  Also report component count against the declared 100 - if it is far below,
  merging is still significant and the seeds are not clean.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. Seeds show the SAME edge softening as grown tiles: near-parity within ~5
   tiles of the boundary, full bias by ~12. Then the weakening is in the SEED
   BIAS FUNCTION itself, Sec.6.2's border band is modelling the right thing, and
   growth is innocent. This is the outcome that closes 11(a) with a mechanism
   located even if not explained.
2. Seeds are uniformly biased regardless of edge distance. Then seeding is
   clean and GROWTH produces the border effect - presumably by clipping against
   the boundary and redistributing - and Sec.6.2's border band is fitted to the
   wrong stage. That would be the more interesting and more expensive result.
3. The raw seed ratio is much HIGHER than the 29:1 measured from grown tiles.
   Expected either way and worth capturing: growth necessarily blurs seeds
   across the line, so grown tiles understate the seeding bias. **This run gives
   the first uncontaminated estimate of the actual weight ratio**, which is the
   number Sec.6.2's weight table should be fitted to rather than 18:1 or 29:1.
4. Component count comes back near 100. Well below ~85 means merging is still
   material and the centroids are not seeds; the run still reads, but every
   figure needs the same caveat the earlier ones carry.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200) WITH ANY PLAYER COUNT - no player lands, so the count
does not enter. RUN IT FIVE TIMES, and REGENERATE BETWEEN SAVES: three separate
batches have now produced duplicate exports, and the check is that the elevated
tile total differs run to run.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

  create_elevation 1
  {
    number_of_clumps 100
    number_of_tiles 600
    spacing 1
  }

/* NO <CLIFF_GENERATION> HEADER - an empty one generates cliffs. */

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
